Trace:

  1. convertToAssets only takes the escrow branch when t_CurrentAccount != address(0) and
  _isWithdrawRequestPending(t_CurrentAccount), i.e. inside a transaction contextualized to that same account — it is not
  a globally-poisoned price feed.
  2. The escrow branch calls ILPLib(LP_LIB).getWithdrawRequestValue(account, asset, shares), which loops over EVERY
  token of the pool, fetches that token's manager and calls manager.getWithdrawRequestValue(vault, account, asset,
  shares), and require(hasRequest) for each one. So a "dust request on one leg" does not even reach a value: the exit in
  __initiateWithdraw is forced proportional (isSingleSided: false), and BaseLPLib.initiateWithdraw skips any leg whose
  exitBalances[i] == 0; if a leg has no manager/request the value call reverts on the require(hasRequest). There is no
  path that silently values the whole position off one partial snapshot.
  3. The manager records sharesAmount: sharesHeld alongside yieldTokenAmount: exitBalances[i], and both the valuation
  (getWithdrawRequestValue(..., shares)) and the redemption (yieldTokensBurned = w.yieldTokenAmount * sharesToRedeem /
  w.sharesAmount in finalizeAndRedeemWithdrawRequest) scale strictly by shares / w.sharesAmount. So pairing a dust
  yieldTokenAmount with a full sharesHeld makes the account's reported value smaller, not larger — the claimed "chosen
  number" is a self-inflicted undervaluation that would get the attacker liquidated and would strand the still-staked LP
  balance (the escrow redeem path never touches _unstakeAndExitPool). It is not a profit path; the direction of the
  error is against the attacker, and the protocol's escrow accounting burns exactly sharesToRedeem against the pro-rata
  request.
  4. Most decisively for the specific mechanism claimed: __initiateWithdraw is internal and override; the
  yieldTokenAmount and sharesHeld arguments are supplied by the base AbstractYieldStrategy external entry point, which
  is NOT in either file shown. Nothing in the reviewed code lets an unprivileged caller choose an arbitrary dust
  yieldTokenAmount while passing full sharesHeld — that mismatch is asserted, not demonstrated, and the deciding code is
  absent from both the abstract file and the derived CurveConvex2Token override set (which only implements NUM_TOKENS,
  PRIMARY_INDEX, TOKENS, _totalPoolSupply, _checkReentrancyContext).

  The claim therefore fails on both the mechanism (the caller-controlled dust amount is unproven and the standard flow
  ties yieldTokenAmount to the redeemed share fraction) and on the economics (the boolean flag routes to a pro-rata
  shares/sharesAmount valuation that under-values rather than over-values a partial request). Under the tie-break rule,
  the unshown entry point alone is sufficient to refuse confidence.

